WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.887
In part one

2
00:00:03.887 --> 00:00:07.177
a matrix did something to every vector.

3
00:00:07.177 --> 00:00:10.220
Now the question is: can we undo it?

4
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.393
A photo app has a filter that

5
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.482
mixes two colour channels, warm and cool.

6
00:00:17.579 --> 00:00:20.305
A pixel with warm w and cool

7
00:00:20.402 --> 00:00:24.588
c becomes three w plus c, and two w plus c.

8
00:00:24.588 --> 00:00:26.730
That is the matrix F.

9
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.521
Watch it act on the plane.

10
00:00:29.521 --> 00:00:32.149
The unit square leans over

11
00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:36.279
and its corners land on three, two and one

12
00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:36.748
one.

13
00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:40.409
The pixel five, two becomes seventeen

14
00:00:40.409 --> 00:00:41.160
twelve.

15
00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.118
We want one matrix that brings

16
00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:45.682
every pixel back.

17
00:00:45.682 --> 00:00:48.898
What must it do to the leaning grid?

18
00:00:48.898 --> 00:00:50.550
Pause and predict.

19
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:58.752
For a two by two matrix there is a formula.

20
00:00:58.752 --> 00:01:03.373
Swap a and d, change the signs of b and c

21
00:01:03.373 --> 00:01:06.490
and divide by a d minus b c.

22
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.855
For F, a d minus b c is three minus two

23
00:01:11.855 --> 00:01:13.611
which is one.

24
00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:19.002
So F inverse is one, minus one, minus two

25
00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:19.880
three.

26
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.571
Now apply F inverse to the leaning grid.

27
00:01:23.571 --> 00:01:25.576
Everything slides back.

28
00:01:25.576 --> 00:01:29.168
The square is square again, and seventeen

29
00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:31.590
twelve returns to five, two.

30
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.291
F inverse times F is the identity

31
00:01:35.291 --> 00:01:38.148
and so is F times F inverse.

32
00:01:38.247 --> 00:01:40.612
A matrix with an inverse

33
00:01:40.711 --> 00:01:42.780
is called invertible.

34
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.675
For bigger matrices we use elimination.

35
00:01:46.675 --> 00:01:48.375
Write the matrix K

36
00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:50.840
and next to it the identity.

37
00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:52.414
Now eliminate

38
00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:55.299
and clear the entries above each

39
00:01:55.386 --> 00:01:57.659
pivot as well as below it.

40
00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.932
Here it takes four steps

41
00:01:59.932 --> 00:02:02.118
row three minus row one

42
00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:05.003
row one minus two times row two

43
00:02:05.003 --> 00:02:07.975
row one plus two times row three

44
00:02:07.975 --> 00:02:10.510
and row two minus row three.

45
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.431
The left half is now the identity

46
00:02:13.431 --> 00:02:15.851
so the right half is K inverse.

47
00:02:15.851 --> 00:02:18.951
This is called Gauss Jordan elimination

48
00:02:18.951 --> 00:02:21.900
and here the answer has whole numbers.

49
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.977
Why does it work?

50
00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:28.140
All the steps together are one matrix E

51
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.883
with E times K equal to I.

52
00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:34.843
So E is K inverse, and the right half

53
00:02:34.843 --> 00:02:37.890
which started as I, became E.

54
00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:40.337
Now apply two filters.

55
00:02:40.337 --> 00:02:43.306
First B, which adds twice the warm

56
00:02:43.391 --> 00:02:46.445
channel to the cool one, and then F.

57
00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:48.990
The whole thing is F times B.

58
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.996
It is tempting to undo it with

59
00:02:52.085 --> 00:02:54.387
F inverse times B inverse.

60
00:02:54.387 --> 00:02:56.600
That is the wrong order

61
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.433
that product gives eleven, two

62
00:02:59.433 --> 00:03:02.266
minus twenty eight, minus five

63
00:03:02.266 --> 00:03:03.860
not the identity.

64
00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:08.777
To undo two steps, undo the last step first.

65
00:03:08.777 --> 00:03:13.448
F was applied last, so F inverse goes first

66
00:03:13.448 --> 00:03:15.524
and then B inverse.

67
00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.846
The inverse of F B is B inverse

68
00:03:18.949 --> 00:03:20.610
times F inverse.

69
00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:24.566
Can every nonzero matrix be undone?

70
00:03:24.566 --> 00:03:24.951
No.

71
00:03:24.951 --> 00:03:26.784
Take a sum filter

72
00:03:26.784 --> 00:03:29.870
both channels become their sum.

73
00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.592
The pixel three, one becomes four, four.

74
00:03:33.592 --> 00:03:35.952
So does the pixel two, two.

75
00:03:36.036 --> 00:03:38.312
In fact every pixel on this

76
00:03:38.397 --> 00:03:40.504
line lands on four, four

77
00:03:40.588 --> 00:03:42.864
and after the filter nobody

78
00:03:42.948 --> 00:03:45.140
can tell which one it was.

79
00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.781
Their difference, one, minus one

80
00:03:48.781 --> 00:03:50.176
goes to zero.

81
00:03:50.176 --> 00:03:52.370
No matrix can undo S.

82
00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.940
Here is another matrix, T: two

83
00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.930
six in the first row, and one

84
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.956
three in the second.

85
00:04:00.956 --> 00:04:04.910
Its columns are two, one and six, three.

86
00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:09.953
Without the formula, find a nonzero x with T

87
00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.407
times x equal to zero.

88
00:04:12.407 --> 00:04:15.820
Pause, and look at the columns.

89
00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.152
Column two is three times column one.

90
00:04:23.152 --> 00:04:26.397
Stretch column one three times

91
00:04:26.397 --> 00:04:30.047
and it lands exactly on column two.

92
00:04:30.047 --> 00:04:32.481
So three of column one

93
00:04:32.481 --> 00:04:35.929
minus one of column two, is zero

94
00:04:35.929 --> 00:04:38.261
x is three, minus one.

95
00:04:38.261 --> 00:04:42.520
And a d minus b c is six minus six, zero.

96
00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.185
This is the general test.

97
00:04:45.185 --> 00:04:48.425
If A x is zero for some nonzero x

98
00:04:48.425 --> 00:04:50.091
A has no inverse.

99
00:04:50.184 --> 00:04:52.406
An inverse would have to

100
00:04:52.498 --> 00:04:54.350
send zero back to x

101
00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.590
but it can only send zero to zero.

102
00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.217
Last, a habit for working with AI.

103
00:05:01.307 --> 00:05:03.996
Suppose an assistant says this

104
00:05:04.085 --> 00:05:06.057
matrix has no inverse

105
00:05:06.057 --> 00:05:10.090
because no row is a multiple of another row.

106
00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.640
That reason is not the test.

107
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.076
The claim happens to be right

108
00:05:15.076 --> 00:05:16.962
for a different reason

109
00:05:16.962 --> 00:05:19.713
row three is row one plus row two

110
00:05:19.713 --> 00:05:22.306
and the vector five, minus four

111
00:05:22.306 --> 00:05:23.720
one goes to zero.

112
00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.757
A right answer with a wrong reason

113
00:05:26.836 --> 00:05:28.812
fails on the next matrix.

114
00:05:28.812 --> 00:05:30.550
Check the reason too.

115
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.270
To sum up.

116
00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:37.398
The inverse undoes a matrix: for two by two

117
00:05:37.398 --> 00:05:42.298
swap, negate, and divide by a d minus b c

118
00:05:42.298 --> 00:05:46.970
in general, Gauss Jordan on A next to I.

119
00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:51.737
To undo a product, undo the last step first.

120
00:05:51.737 --> 00:05:55.852
And if A sends a nonzero vector to zero

121
00:05:55.852 --> 00:05:57.960
there is no inverse.

122
00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:03.048
Now you try: invert the matrix four, three

123
00:06:03.048 --> 00:06:06.905
one, one, and check both products.

124
00:06:06.905 --> 00:06:11.203
Next lecture: the steps of elimination

125
00:06:11.313 --> 00:06:15.390
become a factorization, A equals L U.
